STATUS -- what needed explaining================================Every program on this disk has been run, and the catalogue shows each onerunning. This is the working record behind that: the programs that needed asetup, hit a limit or had a fault, what was measured, and what was done. Aprogram not named here ran without surprises. DOC/INDEX is the roster.Each program has one entry, under the heading that fits it now. A programthat has been fixed has one line under FIXED.--------------------------------------------------------------NEEDS A SETUP--------------------------------------------------------------Run from a login session. SYS/login sets TERM, TERMCAP, PATH, SIMPATH, SHELLand the rest, and a program started without them can fail for no fault ofits own. Setting a variable in the host shell that starts os9exec changesnothing inside it.THE ENVIRONMENT  crib         wants TERM; without it, `Unknown terminal type'.  cribbage     wants TERM; without it, `Environment variable TERM not               defined', then a bus error.  snake        wants TERM; without it, `No TERM in environment'.  aterm        reads its configuration from SYS/ATERM. `aterm /t1' for a               serial port.  gnuchessn    wants TERMCAP to hold the capability string rather than a               file name: `. /dd/SYS/termcap.entry' first. gnuchess and               hexedit the same.  wam.sbprolog  needs SIMPATH=/dd/SBPROLOG/MODLIB, which login sets.  gs403        needs GS_LIB: `export GS_LIB=/dd/LIB/gs403' in bash. `setenv'               is the OS-9 shell's command, and bash answers `command not               found'.  dm           runs the commands on its bottom line through system(), so               SHELL must name a shell that can carry them out. Login's ksh               does.  make         a recipe line holding a `>' or `|' is handed to $SHELL.               Login's ksh runs it; bash cannot. A command line must begin               with a tab, and a tab typed at this terminal is expanded, so               copy DOC/make/demo.mk rather than echoing a makefile.  tex          work through the wrappers with login's SHELL: `latex               SYS/TEX/SAMPLES/small.tex' writes small.dvi. Without SHELL               they return 221 in silence. `virtex "&plain" <file>' (or               `&lplain') runs the engine directly. `slitex' the same.A MODULE LOADED FIRST  rtf          the RTF Fortran set -- rtf, for, lnk, lnk.org, biory, creadoc               -- links `os9lib', and without it exits silently. `load               /dd/CMDS/os9lib'. Other programs carry the string and do not               need it. DOC/README-FORTRAN.  for          forks Microware's `shell' for each compiler pass. Call `rtf'               directly and no shell is wanted. At bash, `for' is also a               keyword.  creadoc      wants os9lib loaded and your own shell, dir and del to hand.               It then reproduces DOC/rtf/biory.doc exactly.  rayshade     builds its scene through popen(), which forks your own OS-9's               `shell' to run `cccp': `load /h1/CMDS/shell' and `load               /dd/CMDS/GCC139/gcc_cccp' first. It then renders.  screen       `screens', the login greeting. It wants a $HOME/.screens               directory, and runs the file it picks through system(), so               your own `shell' must be on the execution path.  printmail    forks `readmsg' by bare name, which resolves against the               execution directory: `load /dd/CMDS/ELM/readmsg' and it works               from anywhere.  rxmod        calls the VMod_trap handler: `load /dd/CMDS/COMMS/vmod_trap'               first. It then waits for a module on its serial line and               gives up with `Timeouted'. (The library's module was named               `vmod_trap'; it is now `VMod_trap', the name rxmod asks for.)  ptxminst     installs Ptxm: `load /dd/CMDS/ptxm' first. See FAULTS.THE DATA DIRECTORYA program that opens a file by bare name reads it from the data directory.ksh's `cd' moves it; at an interactive bash, `cd' does not. So: `ksh -c "cd<dir>; <prog>"'.  orbit        reads kepler.dat, mode.dat and <site>.sit from DOC/orbit:               `ksh -c "cd /dd/DOC/orbit; orbit"'.  advcom       opens its `@objects.adi' include by bare name, and keeps a               filename in 20 characters. Work in a directory of your own               holding the files from GAMES/ADVSYS.  adlcomp      needs `-i<dir>' naming the directory that holds standard.adl,               for any world that includes it: from ADL/DEMOS, `adlcomp               tiny.adl -o tiny -i..'.  advent       opens /dd/GAMES/adv/glorkz by absolute path, so it needs this               disk as /dd.  hack         run it by its full path, /dd/CMDS/GAMES/hack. It changes               directory and then stats argv[0], so a bare name stops it               with `Cannot get status of hack'.  mkdict       run it in /dd/GAMES/BOG, where bog's word list is. From               GAMES/words it makes GAMES/BOG/dict byte for byte. (It took a               bus error on an over-long line until 2026-09-26.)  makecrc      writes arc.c, binhex.c, ccitt.c, ccitt32.c, kermit.c and               zip.c -- CRC table source -- into the data directory, and               prints nothing.  scales       writes scales.lst in the data directory; the screen stays               empty. It wants at least one of -d -a -m -c.  ar           refuses an absolute path: `absolute path illegal'. Name               members relative to the data directory.A RAM DISK AT /r0OS-9's C library builds temporary file paths on /r0, and a number ofprograms here carry one; DOC/README-RUNNING lists them. On a real system /r0is there. Under os9exec, `mount -r=256k /r0' makes one, in a running sessionas well. A search for the path must be case-insensitive: some carry `/R0'.  ed           stops at once with `/r0/ed.<n>: module not found' without               one; with one it opens the file and answers with its size.  mail         `mail: /r0/ma<n> cannot open for writing' without one; with               one it files the message.  hexed        the same shape as ed. Rebuilt trap-free; see FIXED.  pri          chains to `copy -w=/r0/list' run from /r0/cmds, so it wants               your own copy there too. Without it, 221.  ELM/filter   with /r0 it reads its rules and matches them, then fails on               its own bug; SOURCES.txt has it.ARGUMENTS AND INPUTAll the Dhrystone builds read a run count on standard input and wait for it.  queens       reads the board size on standard input, as a number: `echo 5               | queens'.  cvtbase      reads the number on standard input: `echo 255 | cvtbase d b'               prints 11111111.  byteflip     needs its maps: `byteflip 4 0 1 2 3 4 3 2 1 0' reverses every               four-byte word. With none it reads zero bytes for ever.  rechne       one expression, no spaces: `rechne 4095+1'. Spaced out, each               argument is evaluated separately.  rpn          its words are add, sub, mul, div and mod; `?' lists them.               Anything else is taken as a number, so `12 34 +' pushes a 0.  find         takes `-n=<name>' and `-o', not the Unix form, which it               answers with `only one parameter allowed'.  divide       a file splitter: `divide -l=<lines> infile outfile'.  des          takes files and no options; `-e' is read as a filename.  bincheckr    reads a binary opening book, in gnuchess 4.0's layout or the               older one: `bincheckr               /dd/SRC/gnuchess/GNUCHESS4.0/MISC/gnuchess.data'. Given the               text book GAMES/gnuchess.book it reports booksize 0.  checkgame    reads the `chess.lst' gnuchess writes when you type `list',               checks it and prints the final board. Its usage line says               `game'.  game         reads the same chess.lst and writes PostScript, a board for               each move.  tangle       tangle and weave need a change file named. DOC/tex/none.ch is               an empty one.  l            takes files; a directory gets `not accessable, error: 214'.QUIET BY DESIGN  wisecrack    a server: it writes slogans to /pipe/txtpipe and prints               nothing itself. Start it first, `wisecrack &'.  ask          its client: it takes one line from that pipe and prints it.               Alone it says `No Wisecracks coming'.  lpsched      the second print spooler. `lpsched /pipe/prt &' creates the               `spoolqueue' event and data module; then `lp <file>' answers               `Spoolid : 1', `lpq' reports, `lprm' deletes and `lpshut'               stops it. Name your own printer in place of /pipe/prt. Under               os9exec a job never reports done -- prjob exits and lpsched               stays waiting on its event -- so no completion mail is sent.               Which user it mails was fixed 2026-09-26.  splman       the first spooler's manager, and `splprt' its printer               process. Both are daemons and say nothing. /dd/SPL/splq ships               empty, and `splstat' reports it.  fileserv     the UUCP file server: it waits for a request and says               nothing.  readnews     asks its question and waits; here it says `**** End of               newsgroups'.  elvprsv      elvis's crash-recovery helper, not run from the command line               (DOC/elvis/elvprsv.doc).  cron         reads /dd/USR/LIB/crontab, which ships empty, so it schedules               nothing.  bootlogger   opens /dd/SYS/bootlog, which ships empty.  suse         a benchmark that computes and exits; it makes no system call               and prints nothing.  vtxtcn       writes world's .inc files, and needs world's .dat files               present.KEYS  digclk       Control-C changes the language of the date line; Control-E               stops it.  draw         Escape quits; `?' shows the other keys.--------------------------------------------------------------LIMITS--------------------------------------------------------------OF THE MACHINE UNDER os9exec  g            the Atari `Graph' group -- g, striche, apfel, sine, showpic,               graphdemo, graphsave -- needs an Atari ST with a monochrome               monitor. The library, CMDS/GAMES/graph, runs in supervisor               state (M$Attr $A0), and its own manual, DOC/graph/graph.doc,               says it cannot be linked into ordinary programs. `load' it               and each program starts, then takes a privilege violation               (vector $08) on the library's RTE. os9exec runs no guest code               in supervisor state. Source at SRC/graph. (The library's               module was named `graph'; it is now `Graph', the name its               programs ask for.)  cyberwar     G-Windows programs: cyberwar, puzzle and scriptmaster draw               through G-Windows, so at a terminal they print nothing.               Documented in DOC/.  lfmaker      asks its terminal for a G-Windows setstat ($4749), is told               E$UnkSvc, and exits with 208 in silence.  setime       nothing here sets the clock: os9exec's F$STime returns               success and changes nothing. setime, setime2, setimex and               setyear all succeed and leave the date as it was. On a real               system they, and `fixyear', do their job.  getsys       prints the global page. Some globals answer `F$SetSys:               unimplemented' on standard error. They serve machinery an               emulator lacks, and it was settled that they read 0.  infoxpress   opens a serial port, /t3, and names the host terminal.  wysecrack    probes a Wyse terminal; with none on the line it waits.  authwn       WN's authentication helper and `inetdc' need inetd, which               reads Microware's `inetdb' data module from your own               networking.  msntp        wants a network; `xengine' and `basicwin' want an X server.  msformat     refuses the image: `Non-removable media is not supported'. A               real floppy is what it wants. mscopy, msdir and mstype work               on DOS/b.img, drive b: in SYS/mtools.conf.OF THIS C LIBRARYIt has no rename() and no link(). Each program below does its work first andleaves the result under a temporary name; renaming it yourself is the wholeremedy.  zip          writes the archive as _Z<n> and stops with `Could not create               output file'. `mv _Z* mine.zip' and `unzip -t' answers OK.               Use bare names: a stored pathname loses its leading slash.  arc          works with a relative archive name. With an absolute one it               stops at `Cannot move A<n>.arc to <target>'.  autolf       given a filename it converts through a temporary and reports               `couldn't rename'. As a filter it works: `autolf -c -C -L <               in > out' does what todos does, and `autolf -C' what toos9               does.OF BASHSYS/login hands you bash, and these names are also bash builtins orkeywords, so typing the name never reaches the program. Give the path --/dd/CMDS/help -- and each runs.  enable       re-enable a UUCP device (builtin).  fc           re-execute history (builtin).  for          the Fortran driver (keyword).  help         the .hlp help system (builtin).  if           shell-script condition (keyword).  trap         trap-handler demonstration (builtin). Run by path it prints               `**** Can't install trap handler ****' and names the handler               it wanted: it installs from system state, so from an ordinary               program it stops. That is its report.OF OS-9 ITSELFA resident module wins by name, whatever path you type. Two toolchains sharemodule names: gcc and gpp in GCC139 and GCC2, and their passes cc1plus,cccp2, collect and cc2. Load one toolchain, not both. CMDS/gnuchess andCMDS/GAMES/gnuchess are two different ports under one name.BETWEEN TWO SETS OF TOOLS  cjpeg        cjpeg and djpeg write and read PNM headers with LF; netpbm               here writes CR. Each rejects the other's file -- `Bogus data               in PPM file', `junk in file where an integer should be'.               Three bytes separate them, and `pbyte' patches each: for               `ppmmake red 8 8', `pbyte red.ppm 2 0a', `pbyte red.ppm 6               0a', `pbyte red.ppm a 0a', and 0d the other way. The offsets               move with the digits in the dimensions. cjpeg.070 reads CR               and djpeg.070 writes it, so that pair needs no patching.  pgmtopbm     without -threshold dithers differently every run.  ppmtoicr     the paletted converters -- this one, ppmtosixel, ppmtouil,               ppmtopuzz, ppmtopict, ppmtopi1 and others -- write nothing               for a 24-bit image. `ppmquant 16' first.--------------------------------------------------------------FAULTS NOT FIXED--------------------------------------------------------------  cpu          draws its bar chart and its answer, then ends on vector $07,               E_TRAPV (107) -- a double overflow in the maths library, most               likely a rate over an elapsed time of 0. The session goes on.               It also dates itself in the year 19126.  ptxminst     with ptxm loaded it takes a bus error (vector $02) reading a               structure never set up for it: the value fetched, $aaaaae48,               is a fill pattern. DOC/ptxm/ptxm.txt.--------------------------------------------------------------SOURCE THAT WILL NOT BUILD--------------------------------------------------------------  hack         source damaged in all three surviving copies. The binary               works and is in CMDS/GAMES.  phan         18 corrupt bytes across every source file; not               reconstructable without inventing source text.  gammon       gammon and tet2 need BSD sgtty.h and a.out.h, interfaces OS-9               never had.SOURCES.txt has the reasoning.--------------------------------------------------------------FIXED--------------------------------------------------------------  aprocs       prints the process table; os9exec gained the F$SetSys it               wanted.  blackjak     poker, blackjak, rpoem, rstory and stone read their syntax               files from GAMES/SNOBOL, from anywhere.  config       runs to its last line. os9exec installed the program's own               F$STrap handler 64K above where it asked; fixed in os9exec               d401ce2.  cp           byte patch: with no arguments it prints its usage and               stops, where it took a bus error.  devprc       rebuilt from SRC/devprc: the archive copy had a bad CRC and               damaged initialised data. Lists every process's open paths,               bare and with -a.  dir          one byte patched: a MOVEQ #128 that meant 127.  gmake        runs its recipes.  head         one byte patch: it no longer reports every file it read               as an error, and returns 0.  lpq          lpq, lp and lprm find the spooler, since os9exec's F$DatMod               marks its module as data.  lua          lua, runc, msntp, basicwin and xengine load against the csl               here.  m4           the archive's GNU m4 0.5 took the name; the build it replaced               scrambled its output.  makedb       USR/LIB/SMAIL and a starter palias ship.  nroff        rebuilt trap-free; `man' uses it.  oleo         opens on its notice and draws the grid, since os9exec's trap               fixes.  pacman       draws its maze with termcap cursor motion and plays.  patch        opens files with OS-9 modes and patches.  pgmedge      the masked-signal freeze was os9exec's, fixed in 8ee3bcf.  printf       rebuilt trap-free; every literal arrives.  read_mail    MAIL/mail_ ships, and `arepdaemon' has /dd/spool/uucp.  sed          the archive's GNU sed 1.06 took the name; the build it               replaced answered `No more memory'.  snake        rebuilt without an unbuffered stdout.  todos        byte patch: converts in place, through todos.___.<n> and               your own del and rename; no stray line feed.  toos9 the same,               with no trailing 0xFF.  top          draws its process table and returns.  unstr        unstr, cvtbase, etags, cdiff, pagekwic, pagefraz, cookhash,               xrf, unifdef, ape, hexed and valspeak rebuilt trap-free: the               era's cio.l and the cio module disagreed on the putc/getc               selectors. DOC/README-CIO.  yacc         the same os9exec freeze as pgmedge; it writes y.tab.c.